WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.426
Step four. We connect the repository to Azure,

00:00:02.476 --> 00:00:05.334
and we put guardrails on how changes reach production.

00:00:05.384 --> 00:00:08.995
In Microsoft Entra ID, find your own user, and copy the object ID.

00:00:09.045 --> 00:00:13.156
The template uses it to give you access to the cluster views in the portal.

00:00:13.206 --> 00:00:14.396
Copy the Object ID.

00:00:14.446 --> 00:00:17.138
It becomes the admin principal ID setting.

00:00:17.188 --> 00:00:20.741
In the repository settings, create an environment called production.

00:00:20.791 --> 00:00:23.095
Tick Required reviewers, and add yourself.

00:00:23.145 --> 00:00:26.456
Deploy jobs that target production now wait for an approval.

00:00:26.506 --> 00:00:29.588
Here is what this repository ended up with: the production gate,

00:00:29.638 --> 00:00:33.551
five encrypted secrets with the Azure identifiers, and an active ruleset on main.

00:00:34.601 --> 00:00:36.321
The same settings exist as code.

00:00:36.371 --> 00:00:39.585
This Terraform file declares the environment, its reviewer,

00:00:39.635 --> 00:00:41.356
and the main-only branch policy.

00:00:41.406 --> 00:00:46.103
And the ruleset: pull requests, eleven required status checks, no force pushes,

00:00:46.153 --> 00:00:47.064
and no deletion.

00:00:47.114 --> 00:00:50.153
The workflows read the identifiers from variables or secrets,

00:00:50.203 --> 00:00:53.850
and a first job checks that Azure is connected before anything else runs.
